   227	
   228	33 of them are type errors and our method detects 29 of
   229	the type errors (i.e., 87.9% recall). Our tool reports 14 false
   230	positives due to its lack of path-sensitivity. The remaining
   231	57-33=24 bugs are mostly due to pure math incorrectness
   232	and beyond type systems. We also apply our technique in a
   233	most recent audit contest organized by Code4Rena [ 2 ] for a
   234	very large project with more than ten thousand lines of code
   235	and find six zero-day accounting bugs in nine contracts that
   236	we check. Four can lead to direct fund loss. We have included
   237	
   238	our tool and benchmarks in the supplementary document.
   239	We will release the tool upon publication.
   240	
   241	
   242	**2** **MOTIVATION**
   243	
   244	
   245	We use two real-world accounting bugs to explain the inadequacy
   246	of existing techniques and illustrate our method.
   247	
   248	
   249	**Example I (Lending Contract Vader).** Figure 1 contains code
   250	detailing two functions included within the _Pools_ contract from the
   251	_Vader_ project [ 15 ]. They have been shortened for demonstrative
   252	purposes. Vader is a lending project that enables the pooling of
   253	funds and offers borrowing functionalities. Users can participate by
   254	purchasing shares from a liquidity pool and utilize these shares as
   255	collateral to access borrowed funds, thereby increasing the pool’s
   256	returns. Within the project, The Pools contract stores functions
   257	related the movement of liquidity in the pool.
   258	The function addLiquidity() in Figure 1 converts a contract’s
   259	recently added _base_ currency and _token_ currency into liquidity,
   260	and adds the liquidity to a certain member ’s account. In the contract, the base currency is the default currency of the Vader project,
   261	while the token currency is a special currency used by the liquidity pool, representing a share of the pool. The amount of recently added base currency is computed on line 4 as addedBase,
   262	and the amount of recently added token is computed on line 5
   263	as addedToken . The equivalent amount of liquidity is calculated
   264	through the calcLiquidityUnits() function call on line 6, in
   265	which totalBase, totalToken, and totalLiquidity denote the
   266	total amount of base currency, total token currency, and total liquidity in the pool. The resulting liquidity is then added to the member
   267	account on line 7 and the total base currency is updated on line 8.
